  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThrough Tonight...A band of light, isolated showers extending \nacross the local Atlantic waters and towards the Treasure Coast \nwill slowly diminish through the remainder of the afternoon, with \nmostly dry conditions elsewhere. Late this evening, a weakening \ncold front draped across the Big Bend region will push southward \ntowards the peninsula, with a low chance (20-30%) of isolated \nshower development out ahead of the front. Rain chances will \nexpand from the northwest towards the southeast as the front moves\nthrough the area. No other hazards outside of showers are \nanticipated, with lightning chances remaining below 20% at this \ntime. Temperatures remain on track to peak in the mid 80s areawide\nthis afternoon, cooling into the mid 60s to low 70s tonight. \nThere is a low chance of fog development out ahead of the front, \nbut confidence in this remains too low at this time to include \nwithin the forecast. Will continue to monitor closely and adjust \nas needed.\n\nSaturday-Thursday...The weakening cold front will continue \ndrifting southward across the Florida peninsula on Saturday, with \nhigh pressure building behind the front through the remainder of \nthe weekend and into early next week. The boundary will stall \nacross south Florida and the Keys through early to mid next week. \nDrier air sweeps in behind the front locally, keeping rain chances\nbelow 10% Saturday through Tuesday. Temperatures through this \ntimeframe will also trend closer to normal overall, with some \nspots towards the Treasure Coast still staying a touch above \nnormal. Mid-week, a shortwave trough looks to sweep across the \nsoutheastern US and towards Florida, prompting a gradual shift in\nthe weather pattern locally. Current guidance indicates a weak \nlow developing southwest of the Florida peninsula along the \nstalled boundary mid-week, with the energy aloft helping to lift \nthe low northward across the Florida peninsula as the high \npressure moves offshore across the Atlantic and weakens. This \nwould result in increasing moisture across east central Florida \nmid to late week with a return of rain and even isolated storm \nchances, highest across southern portions of the forecast area \nstarting as early as Wednesday. There remains a good bit of \nuncertainty, however, in how the low will evolve, timing of the \nshortwave aloft and where it will develop, so changes in the \nforecast are likely as time moves forward. Temperatures through \nthe extended are currently forecast to trend cooler, even falling \na bit below normal late next week.\n\nWhile the weather looks to be favorable through this weekend and \ninto early next week, there does remain some concerns along the \ncoast. A non-tropical low hundreds of miles south of Bermuda will \ncontinue to send long-period swells towards the east central \nFlorida coast through this weekend and into next week. This will \nsupport a persistent high risk of rip currents over the next \nseveral days, meaning entering the ocean will continue to be \nstrongly discouraged. Additionally, a return of some higher \nastronomical tides is anticipated through the weekend. While this \nevent will not be as significant as the prior event, Martin County\nin particular could see some minor coastal flooding in spots \nduring periods of high tide, especially on Sunday and Monday.",
